From 0534b05312024abdf3d6d887860d4a619ddb98ca Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alexander Penev <alexander.p.penev@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 12:02:33 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] compiler: f64 SIMD lanes, and the answer to where the cheap
 type pays

compile_scene_sdf_simd_f64 emits the vector path in double lanes. One
emitter serves both types - detail::emit_simd differs only in CgCtx::scalar
and the element type - so the two vector paths cannot describe different
solids, the same reason emit_scene_sdf_f64 shares a walk with its f32 twin.

THE OPEN QUESTION IS NOW CLOSED. mixed_eval.hpp said f32 buys no speed on
this CPU and that GPU/SIMD was where it should pay but had not been
measured. Measured, on AVX-512 with 16 float lanes:

    f32 x16   0.73 ns/point
    f64 x8    1.57 ns/point   same register bytes   f32 2.03-2.14x
    f64 x16   1.73 ns/point   same lane count       f32 2.23-2.35x

Register-for-register is the comparison that means something - f32 at 8
lanes against f64 at 8 lanes would be comparing how much work a call does,
not the types - and it is the one that lands on the factor the mechanism
predicts. Four runs, 2.03-2.14x.

So the whole picture, three paths measured:

    interpreted        f32 1.13-1.78x SLOWER  (double store narrows per access)
    compiled scalar    equal within variance  (0.81-1.01x over nine runs)
    compiled SIMD      f32 2.03-2.14x FASTER

The rule is not "f32 is cheap" or "f32 is not cheap": f32 pays exactly where
it buys LANES and nowhere else. Scalar code puts one value in a register
whatever its width, which is why both the interpreter and the scalar JIT see
nothing. That is what makes Lo = float right for a vector cull and
Hi = double right for a reported distance - the split MixedEval exists to
express, now with a measurement behind each half.

Also corrected: the previous commit quoted the scalar band as 0.97-0.99x
from three runs. Nine runs give 0.81-1.01x. Same conclusion, honest width.

Accuracy carries over unchanged: the f32 lanes match the f32 interpreter to
4.9e-04 mm and the f64 lanes match evalw to 9.1e-13 mm, each to what its own
type supports rather than to a shared number.

